\section{Introduction}{

OmicFlow is constructed around the main superclass \link{omics} that in turn is
inherited by \link{metagenomics} and \link{proteomics}, that offer additional
fields or functions. The superclass \link{omics} contains both public and private
methods, only the public componenents are documented, whereas the private
methods can be accessed via \code{metagenomics$private_methods}, which is not supported.

The \link{omics} class components use \link{Matrix} and \link{data.table} in the
background for fast loading and data wrangling, these data structures return
the data by reference, so does the R6 class, therefore classes in OmicFlow do
not create copies of the object unless called via \code{$copy()}.

All classes needs to be first initialised via the \code{$new()} method and
require the \code{metaData} and \code{countData} (or \code{biomData} in case of \code{metagenomics})
field components. Alternatively, you can also initialise with only the \code{metaData}
and later add the other fields via the active binding, but this will not create
a back-up and is not advised!
For more hands-on examples see \href{https://agusinac.github.io/OmicFlow/articles/getting-started.html}{Getting Started with OmicFlow}.

\subsection{Additional arguments in \code{metagenomics$new()}}{
The \code{metagenomics} class also offers the loading of a phylogenetic tree as
a \code{phylo} class (see \link[ape]{as.phylo}) that can be supplied to \code{treeData}.
When a \code{treeData} is supplied than every class field is arranged according to
the tree tip labels.

The \code{biomData} argument expects a \href{http://biom-format.org/}{BIOM v2.1.0.} in either
HDF5 or JSON format. The \code{biomData} must be supplied via a filepath and required
contents are checked upfront, such as the existence of \code{
"/observation/matrix/data",
"/observation/matrix/indptr",
"/observation/matrix/indices",
"/observation/metadata/taxonomy",
"/observation/ids",
"/sample/ids"
}.
}
}

\section{Input requirements}{

Every class in OmicFlow requires at least a \code{metaData} and \code{countData} field to
be specified. The \code{metaData} must contain a \code{SAMPLE_ID} and optionally a \code{SAMPLEPAIR_ID},
see section "Metadata validation" below.

The \code{countData} can be both a matrix or data frame. If the original contains
rownames, then these will become the \code{FEATURE_ID} in \code{featureData} (automatically created),
otherwise a random feature identifier is assigned. If the \code{countData} is a filetype
or a data frame with both matching sample identifiers in \code{SAMPLE_ID} and other
additional columns, then these will be automatically split, columns matching the
\code{SAMPLE_ID} will be placed in the \code{countData}, any other columns will be placed
in front of the \code{FEATURE_ID} column in \code{featureData}.
}

\section{Metadata validation}{

Every class in OmicFlow begins by validating the sample metadata.
This validation checks that sample identifiers match the sample IDs
present in the abundance tables.

The validation rules are defined in JSON format in the omics class.
Metadata can be supplied as either:
\itemize{
\item A CSV or TSV file.
\item A \code{data.table}.
}

In both cases, metadata must include a header row and follow a
one-row-per-sample structure. For file inputs, the header must be
on the first line. Columns not described below are permitted and
are ignored during metadata validation.

\subsection{Minimum requirement}{
The metadata must contain a \code{SAMPLE_ID} column.

\itemize{
\item Each row must have a unique, non-empty sample identifier.
\item Sample identifiers cannot contain spaces; use underscores (\verb{_}) or
dashes (\code{-}) instead.
}

Example metadata:

\preformatted{
SAMPLE_ID  SAMPLEPAIR_ID  CONTRAST_Treatment  VARIABLE_Age
S1         P1             Drug                42
S2         P1             Placebo             36
S3         P2             Drug                51
}
}

\subsection{Column types and naming rules}{

\strong{Required column}

\tabular{lll}{
Column \tab Type \tab Rules \cr
\code{SAMPLE_ID} \tab string \tab Unique, non-empty, no spaces; one identifier
per sample row
}

\strong{Optional standard columns}

\tabular{lll}{
Column \tab Type \tab Rules \cr
\code{SAMPLEPAIR_ID} \tab string \tab Optional; no spaces. Used for samples that
are paired or originate from the same individual or subject.
}
Some functions require a \code{SAMEPLEPAIR_ID} in the \code{metaData} when \code{paired = TRUE} is used.
The user can add or remove the \code{SAMPLEPAIR_ID} at anytime to the \code{metaData} via active binding.

\strong{Pattern-based columns}

Additional variables can be defined with special column-name prefixes:

\itemize{
\item \code{CONTRAST_...}: Grouping or category labels for differential
comparisons. For example, \code{CONTRAST_Treatment} may contain \code{Drug} and
\code{Placebo}.
\item \code{VARIABLE_...}: Numeric or string variables for statistical
analysis. For example, \code{VARIABLE_Age} may contain values such as \code{42} and
\code{51}.
}
}
Pattern-based columns are only used in \href{#method-autoFlow}{\code{autoFlow()}}. Currently, only columns
with the \code{CONTRAST_} prefix are supported.
}

